1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:05.120
[S01] The modern Shakespeare is doom scrolling.

2
00:00:05.129 --> 00:00:11.160
[S01] The modern Nikola Tesla is crap posting on Twitter right now.

3
00:00:11.939 --> 00:00:14.910
[S01] Constant entertainment, it kills original thought.

4
00:00:15.419 --> 00:00:21.149
[S01] You know, if your brain is always in consume mode, like I'm talking every second of the day,

5
00:00:21.149 --> 00:00:25.140
[S01] you're either on your phone, even when you're in your shower, you need music to stimulate you.

6
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:29.820
[S01] When you're just constantly consuming, you don't produce, right?

7
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:33.420
[S01] And creativity, it comes from gaps, not noise.

8
00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:39.109
[S01] A lot of the best ideas that I've had for videos that I've made have literally come

9
00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.950
[S01] from me trying to sleep at night and then I'll randomly have a thought pop into my brain.

10
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.890
[S01] And so I throw it on my notes app and then I record it the next day.

11
00:00:48.149 --> 00:00:51.479
[S01] But boredom is the birthplace of ideas.

12
00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.100
[S01] So some of the best ideas, they don't come when you're stimulated.

13
00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.679
[S01] They come when you're, you know, staring at a wall, when you're sort of walking aimlessly,

14
00:01:01.679 --> 00:01:02.729
[S01] just doing nothing.

15
00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:07.829
[S01] I've said this before, but I think like one of the greatest human experiences is

16
00:01:07.829 --> 00:01:09.329
[S01] directionless driving.

17
00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:14.310
[S01] When you just get in your car, you have no, you're, you're, you're driving for the sake

18
00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:19.109
[S01] of driving and you just kind of leave and then do whatever you want with your life.

19
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:25.950
[S01] Um, you know, you could just put the windows down and just, you know, breathe in the fresh

20
00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:30.569
[S01] air, nature, bonus points if it's at night, you know, warm weather.

21
00:01:31.109 --> 00:01:38.969
[S01] Because what happens with that is you're forced to think, you're forced to just, you

22
00:01:38.969 --> 00:01:46.260
[S01] know, experience the world and try to, um, you know, leave your mark on it as opposed

23
00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.099
[S01] to, you know, watching other, other people do it.

24
00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:58.319
[S01] There's a scene in Game of Thrones where Samwell Tarly, he says, "I'm tired of reading

25
00:01:58.319 --> 00:01:59.579
[S01] books about great men.

26
00:01:59.579 --> 00:02:00.870
[S01] I want to be a great man."

27
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.530
[S01] And I really like that quote because I think it's so true.

28
00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:11.580
[S01] Everybody just sort of pays attention and puts their energy towards watching other

29
00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.830
[S01] people do things and they hardly ever do it themselves.

30
00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.599
[S01] I've said this before on my channel, but I believe there are two people in this world,

31
00:02:18.599 --> 00:02:20.280
[S01] the amazing and the amazed.

32
00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:26.629
[S01] The amazing watch, or the amazed watch the amazing go out in the world and do something.

33
00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:31.110
[S01] There are, you know, there's the person in the arena and then there's the spectator

34
00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:37.289
[S01] in the stands and it's like 90, 98, 99% of people are the spectators.

35
00:02:37.289 --> 00:02:42.870
[S01] They're the amazed and only 1% are the, the people in the arena, the amazing.

36
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.590
[S01] And I think one of the biggest ways that you can tell or differentiate is who's actually

37
00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:54.840
[S01] trying to create something, who's doing, who is actually materializing and then who

38
00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.240
[S01] is just a critic, right?

39
00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.830
[S01] They don't make statues of critics.

40
00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:03.210
[S01] So your brain needs that idle time to connect the dots.

41
00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.430
[S01] You know, when you're bored, your mind naturally starts linking thoughts.

42
00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:15.990
[S01] It starts, you know, tapping into old memories and ideas, um, in ways that it simply can't

43
00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:17.129
[S01] when it's distracted.

44
00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:23.219
[S01] I think this is one of the biggest tragedies of, uh, modern technology is that we found

45
00:03:23.219 --> 00:03:29.009
[S01] a way to constantly give ourselves tiny dopamine hits and then trick us out of taking action.

46
00:03:30.419 --> 00:03:32.849
[S01] People, people call it success porn.

47
00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.469
[S01] It's like when you watch a war edit on your phone, all of a sudden you get this, this

48
00:03:37.469 --> 00:03:42.150
[S01] adrenaline and this dopamine hit as though like you were out in the world protecting

49
00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:49.139
[S01] families, you know, being, uh, being physical, you know, conquering like all this stuff.

50
00:03:49.139 --> 00:03:53.520
[S01] It's like you literally feel as though you're doing it and that you don't have to go out

51
00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.110
[S01] in the world and actually try something.

52
00:04:03.449 --> 00:04:10.979
[S01] Entertainment is easy, creation is uncomfortable and that's the biggest reason why like people

53
00:04:10.979 --> 00:04:12.240
[S01] don't do it, you know?

54
00:04:12.569 --> 00:04:20.329
[S01] Quick hits of stimulation, lower your tolerance, really for effort, but just in general, lower

55
00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:21.240
[S01] your tolerance.

56
00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:30.019
[S01] So inventing, creating, you know, thinking deeply, that stuff is just, it's, it's too,

57
00:04:30.389 --> 00:04:33.480
[S01] it requires too much effort and so people, they don't, they just don't want to do it

58
00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:33.930
[S01] anymore.

59
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.079
[S01] What we've done is we've engineered boredom out of our lives.

60
00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.220
[S01] So you, you don't have to like just sit there in silence anymore.

61
00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.569
[S01] People don't even want to read books.

62
00:04:45.569 --> 00:04:51.930
[S01] Instead they just rather have some sort of adaptation made of a book and enjoy that.

63
00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.019
[S01] And with AI getting really good, who knows?

64
00:04:55.019 --> 00:04:59.189
[S01] Maybe AI could just start mass producing TV shows and movies.

65
00:05:04.079 --> 00:05:09.579
[S02] I think another negative side of so much dopamine is that it starts to numb your creativity.

66
00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:16.060
[S02] So quick hits of stimulation, they lower your tolerance for effort.

67
00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:30.529
[S02] There's a required patience, there is a craft that needs to be created.

68
00:05:30.959 --> 00:05:35.569
[S02] Through a journey, through the hero's journey to create something beautiful.

69
00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:42.879
[S02] Vincent van Gogh could never have painted The Starry Night or any of his great works

70
00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:45.980
[S02] had he not gone through so much suffering.

71
00:05:46.449 --> 00:05:53.750
[S02] Fyodor Dostoevsky, the greatest author of all time, would never have written his great novels

72
00:05:53.779 --> 00:05:56.560
[S02] had he not been sent to a Siberian war camp.

73
00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:01.230
[S02] You know, had he not been exiled for many years.

74
00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:09.569
[S02] This is the truth about art, about creation, is that you must go through a journey.

75
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.579
[S02] But people are not willing to go through that journey, instead they'd rather just watch

76
00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:17.939
[S02] somebody go through a journey and then make it feel as though they've done it themselves.

77
00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:29.790
[S02] When you think about it, people being performative online a lot of times is just, people see,

78
00:06:29.819 --> 00:06:36.160
[S02] get inspired by something, oftentimes a movie or some sort of medium where

79
00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:41.870
[S02] an individual did something, like started from nothing, built their way up and then created something.

80
00:06:42.199 --> 00:06:46.139
[S02] And then, you know, people like a certain aspect of that and they start to

81
00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:52.649
[S02] apply it to their life or like make it, you know, their bio or

82
00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:57.920
[S02] you know, dress a certain way that this person did when they had to go on this journey to get there.

83
00:06:57.949 --> 00:07:04.019
[S02] And instead, everybody just wants to have that cool aesthetic without, you know, putting in what's required

84
00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:09.430
[S02] to put yourself in the headspace to, you know, find that aesthetic for yourself.

85
00:07:12.199 --> 00:07:17.769
[S02] Boredom essentially forces self-reflection and that's why I think you must prioritize boredom.

86
00:07:17.899 --> 00:07:23.180
[S02] Because without distractions, you really just start to think about your life, you start to

87
00:07:23.709 --> 00:07:27.410
[S02] become more honest with yourself, your goals, your problems, stuff like that.

88
00:07:27.879 --> 00:07:30.649
[S02] And that's really where the real change begins.

89
00:07:34.889 --> 00:07:38.360
[S02] If you can't be alone with your thoughts, I mean, that's a real problem.

90
00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:45.100
[S02] Needing constant stimulation might be a sign that you are, you know, avoiding something deeper.

91
00:07:48.399 --> 00:07:53.110
[S02] We must spend time alone with ourselves so that we can find out who we truly are.

92
00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:58.110
[S02] And after we find out who we truly are, our identity, then we can create

93
00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:02.920
[S02] from your identity, from the core of who you truly are.

94
00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.160
[S02] So yeah, man, I'll leave you on this, I mean, you don't need more content.

95
00:08:13.329 --> 00:08:17.899
[S02] Most of the content, including mine, is just repurposed from a book or some other

96
00:08:19.329 --> 00:08:22.300
[S02] form of expression.

97
00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:25.509
[S02] You need more space is what you need.

98
00:08:26.069 --> 00:08:32.350
[S02] You're not lacking inspiration, you're just, you know, lacking the silence that's required to hear your own ideas.

99
00:08:33.879 --> 00:08:34.950
[S02] Thank you guys for watching.
